<h1>Feature Contracts at Inference: Train/Serve Skew and the Last-Bar Problem</h1>
<div class="why"><strong>Why this matters here:</strong> your model bundles are saved as
<code>{"model": ..., "features": [...]}</code> and CLAUDE.md warns that the
<code>features</code> list "defines exact column order at inference." This lesson explains
what that contract really is, how <code>build_features</code> silently eats the one row you
most want to predict on, and why <code>strategies/gbm_adapter.py</code> pads the frame with a
copy of the last bar.</div>

<h2 id="s1">1 · The concept: a feature contract</h2>
<p>When you train a model on a table of features, you implicitly sign a contract with your
future self. At inference time you must reproduce:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Clause</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What breaks if violated</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Names</strong></td><td>The same columns exist</td><td><code>KeyError</code>, or silently missing features filled with garbage</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Order</strong></td><td>Columns feed the model in training order</td><td>No error at all — sklearn sees a bare numpy array and happily multiplies RSI by the coefficient it learned for volume. Predictions are nonsense.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Semantics</strong></td><td>Each column is computed the same way</td><td>Subtle drift: RSI via rolling mean ≠ RSI via EMA (Wilder). Same name, different number.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Availability</strong></td><td>Every input is knowable at decision time</td><td>Lookahead bias (using tomorrow) or inference lag (using yesterday when today exists)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Violating any clause produces <strong>train/serve skew</strong>: the model runs on inputs
distributionally different from what it was trained on. The insidious part is that clauses
2–4 usually fail <em>silently</em> — the code runs, numbers come out, and the equity curve
quietly rots.</p>

<h2 id="s2">2 · Minimal standalone example</h2>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># The last-bar problem in eight lines.</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd

close = pd.Series([100, 102, 101, 105], name=<span class="st">"close"</span>)
df = pd.DataFrame({<span class="st">"close"</span>: close})
df[<span class="st">"ret_1d"</span>]     = df[<span class="st">"close"</span>].pct_change()   <span class="cm"># backward-looking: fine today</span>
df[<span class="st">"fwd_ret_1d"</span>] = df[<span class="st">"ret_1d"</span>].shift(-1)      <span class="cm"># the TARGET: tomorrow's return</span>

<span class="cm"># Row 3 (today, close=105) has fwd_ret_1d = NaN — tomorrow hasn't happened.</span>
<span class="cm"># That is CORRECT for training: you can't learn from an unknown label.</span>
clean = df.dropna()   <span class="cm"># ...but dropna() just deleted TODAY —</span>
                      <span class="cm"># the only row you actually want to predict on.</span></code></pre>
<p>The target column <em>must</em> be NaN on the newest bar (anything else is lookahead).
A blanket <code>dropna()</code> is right for building a training set and wrong for building
an inference row. One function serving both callers is where the bug lives.</p>

<h2 id="s3">3 · In this project</h2>

<p><span class="path">paper_trading_sim.py:48–69 — the contract's single source of truth</span>
<code>build_features()</code> produces <code>close, ret_1d, ma_5, ma_10, ma_gap, vol_10,
rsi_14</code> plus the target <code>fwd_ret_1d = ret_1d.shift(-1)</code>, then ends with
<code>return out.dropna()</code>. Every feature is backward-looking; only the target looks
forward. So on any frame, the final returned row is at best <em>yesterday</em>.</p>

<p><span class="path">predict_next.py:136–144 — a live one-day inference lag (pre-existing quirk)</span></p>
<pre><code>feats = build_features(df).dropna()
<span class="cm"># ...</span>
latest = feats.iloc[-1]   <span class="cm"># ← looks like "today", is actually YESTERDAY:</span>
                          <span class="cm">#   build_features' dropna removed today's row</span>
                          <span class="cm">#   because today's TARGET is NaN.</span></code></pre>
<p>The production predictor therefore forecasts tomorrow using features as of the most
recent <em>complete</em> bar — a full day stale. Availability clause violated in the
conservative direction: no cheating, but the model trades on old information.
(Found during the 2026-07-05 overnight session; deliberately not changed there because it
alters live behaviour — it deserves its own reviewed fix.)</p>

<p><span class="path">strategies/gbm_adapter.py — the padding fix used by the Strategy Arena</span></p>
<pre><code>padded = pd.concat([df, df.iloc[[-1]]])   <span class="cm"># duplicate the newest bar</span>
feats = build_features(padded)</code></pre>
<p>After padding, the <em>real</em> newest bar is no longer last, so its target becomes a
finite dummy (return vs. its own copy = 0) and it survives the dropna. The copy, now the
final row, inherits the NaN target and is dropped instead. Because every feature only
looks backward, the copy cannot contaminate the real bar's inputs. Result: true
same-day features from the <em>unmodified</em> production pipeline — the semantics clause
holds because there is still exactly one implementation of the features.</p>

<p><span class="path">strategies/gbm_adapter.py — honouring the order clause</span></p>
<pre><code>X = row[self._feat_cols].astype(float).values.reshape(1, -1)
yhat = float(self._model.predict(X)[0])</code></pre>
<p><code>self._feat_cols</code> comes from the bundle's <code>features</code> list — never
from "whatever columns the frame has." Reindexing by the saved list is what enforces
name + order every single time.</p>

<h2 id="s4">4 · Often confused with</h2>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Lookahead bias</th><th>Inference lag</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>What leaks</strong></td><td>Future information leaks <em>into</em> features/decisions</td><td>Nothing leaks — features are merely <em>stale</em></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Effect on backtest</strong></td><td>Backtest looks <em>better</em> than reality (the killer)</td><td>Backtest and live both look slightly <em>worse</em> than necessary</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direction of error</strong></td><td>Optimistic — you will lose real money finding out</td><td>Pessimistic/conservative — you leave edge on the table</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>In this repo</strong></td><td>Guarded by <code>shift(-1)</code> targets and walk-forward retraining cutoffs (<code>f.index &lt; dt</code>)</td><td><code>predict_next.py</code> predicting off yesterday's row</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Both are violations of the same <em>availability</em> clause — in opposite directions.
Lesson 0032 covered lookahead in candlestick detection; this is its mirror image.
Separately, don't confuse <strong>train/serve skew</strong> (inputs differ between training
and inference) with <strong>overfitting</strong> (model memorises noise in training data).
A perfectly regularised model still fails under skew, and the arena's
backtest-vs-live divergence column is designed to surface exactly that.</p>

