<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q2.</strong> Why is <code>fwd_ret_1d</code> NaN on the newest bar of every frame
  <code>build_features</code> sees?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="yfinance delivers complete OHLCV rows; the NaN is created by build_features itself, not the data source.">Because yfinance hasn't finished downloading the latest row</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="rsi_14 needs 14 bars of history, so it's NaN at the START of the frame, not the end.">Because RSI needs 14 bars of warmup</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — the target is ret_1d.shift(-1): tomorrow's return. For the newest bar, tomorrow doesn't exist yet, and any finite value there would be lookahead.">Because the target is tomorrow's return, and tomorrow hasn't happened</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Rolling windows make the OLDEST rows NaN. The newest bar's backward-looking features are all computable.">Because rolling windows make the newest row incomplete</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q3.</strong> In <code>gbm_adapter.py</code>, why can't the duplicated last bar
  contaminate the real last bar's features?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The copy is NOT removed before computation — it's present during build_features and dropped after, by dropna. The protection comes from direction, not removal order.">Because the copy is removed before features are computed</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct — every feature (returns, MAs, vol, RSI) is computed from bars at or before its own row. The copy sits AFTER the real bar, so it can only influence rows after itself — and it is itself dropped.">Because every feature only looks backward, and the copy sits after the real bar</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="build_features has no dedup logic; duplicate index entries flow straight through rolling computations.">Because pandas deduplicates repeated index dates automatically</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The dummy target on the real bar is 0, and it IS in the frame — it's just never used as a model input, which is a different safeguard.">Because the copy's target is NaN, so its features are ignored</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q4.</strong> Your arena report shows a strategy whose live Sharpe collapses far
  below its backtest Sharpe. Feature code is identical in both paths. Which contract clause
  is the FIRST suspect?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Same build_features implementation in both paths means semantics are identical by construction here.">Semantics — the indicator formulas drifted</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Yes — identical code can still see different DATA timing: the backtest predicts on same-day rows while live (like predict_next.py) may run on yesterday's row, or on partial/unsettled bars. Availability skew is invisible in code review; you find it by comparing decision timestamps.">Availability — live decisions use rows from a different time than backtest decisions</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="A name violation crashes loudly with KeyError — it doesn't produce a quietly worse Sharpe.">Names — a feature column went missing in live</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Possible in general, but the bundle's features list pins order in both paths here; and order bugs usually destroy performance entirely rather than degrade it.">Order — columns swapped between the two paths</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q5.</strong> Lookahead bias vs. inference lag — which statement is true?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backwards: lookahead INFLATES backtests. Lag deflates live edge but doesn't flatter the backtest.">Inference lag inflates backtest results; lookahead deflates them</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="predict_next.py's quirk is lag (stale inputs), not lookahead — no future data enters any feature.">predict_next.py's iloc[-1] quirk is a lookahead bias</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Exactly. Lookahead = optimistic error (backtest lies to you, costs real money later). Lag = conservative error (you trade on old info, leaving edge unused). Same availability clause, opposite directions.">Lookahead makes results look too good; lag merely makes them stale and conservative</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="They're both violations of the availability clause of the feature contract — that's precisely why they're worth contrasting.">They are unrelated problems from different contract clauses</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<h2 id="s6">6 · Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>
<div class="scenario"><details><summary><strong>Scenario A.</strong> You add a <code>news_sent_3d</code> feature and retrain. Six months later a teammate "optimises" news_features.py to use a 5-day window but keeps the column name. What breaks, and would any test catch it?</summary>
<p>The <em>semantics</em> clause breaks: same name, different number, so the saved bundle
silently runs on a distribution it never trained on. Nothing crashes. A characterisation
test pinning known input → known feature values (like the golden values in
test_paper_trading.py) is the only automatic catch; otherwise it surfaces as unexplained
live-vs-backtest divergence in the arena report.</p></details></div>
<div class="scenario"><details><summary><strong>Scenario B.</strong> To "fix" the last-bar problem someone changes build_features to <code>fillna(0)</code> the target instead of dropping. Why is that worse than the padding trick?</summary>
<p>Every training set now contains a final row whose label claims "tomorrow's return was
exactly 0%" — a fabricated label fed into every retrain (including walkforward_sim's
rolling retrains, which call the same function). The padding trick keeps the fabricated
value <em>outside</em> the training path because it only exists in the inference call's
frame, and even there it's never used as an input.</p></details></div>
<div class="scenario"><details><summary><strong>Scenario C.</strong> The FPGA/C++ ONNX path (gradient_boosting/) re-implements feature computation in C++. Which clauses are at risk and what's the cheapest guard?</summary>
<p>Semantics (two implementations of RSI/MA can drift) and order (the ONNX input tensor is
positional). Cheapest guard: a golden-file test — one fixed OHLCV frame, feature vectors
computed by both Python and C++, asserted equal to tolerance. CLAUDE.md's rule "re-export
ONNX after changing build_features" exists exactly because of this risk.</p></details></div>

